  "response": "## Scope\n\n`crimes scan src/api/state.ts --format json` returns exactly **one** `singular_plural_type_mismatch` finding — `crime_00023` (severity `low`, confidence `0.70`, lines 24–27) — but it bundles **two flagged declarations**. Both are covered below.\n\nOne thing to flag before the proposals: `src/api/state.ts:1-2` says\n\n```\n// Intentionally bad: boolean-naming and plural-mismatch demonstrations.\n// This file is fixture material — do not import it from real code.\n```\n\nand `grep` confirms nothing imports this module. So these two declarations exist *to* trip the detector, and \"fixing\" them removes the fixture's positive cases. The analysis below is what I'd recommend if this were production code; if the fixture is the point, the correct action is neither (a) nor (b) — it's to leave both lines alone. (Don't reach for `allowedNames` either: allowlisting these names would blind the detector in real code too.)\n\n---\n\n## Declaration 1 — `users` @ L24\n\nEvidence, verbatim from the finding:\n\n> `plural name, singular type: \\`users: User\\` @L24`\n\nThe code:\n\n```ts\nexport const users: User = { id: \"u_1\", email: \"a@b.c\" };\n```\n\n**The type carries the intended meaning.** The initializer is a single object literal with one `id` and one `email` — there is no collection here, present or implied. `User` is describing the value accurately; the plural `s` is the lie. So this wants option (a), a rename.\n\n**But the obvious rename is blocked.** `src/api/state.ts:33` already declares `export const user: User = { id: \"u_2\", email: \"x@y.z\" };` in the same module scope (it's one of the file's negative controls). `users` → `user` is a redeclaration error, not a fix. Pick a name that says *which* user:\n\n```ts\nexport const currentUser: User = { id: \"u_1\", email: \"a@b.c\" };\n```\n\nOption (b) — widening to `users: User[]` and wrapping the literal in `[...]` — is only right if downstream code was ever meant to iterate. Nothing imports this module, so there's no caller evidence either way, and the single-record initializer is the only signal available. It points at the name being wrong.\n\n---\n\n## Declaration 2 — `invoice` @ L27\n\nEvidence, verbatim from the finding:\n\n> `singular name, array type: \\`invoice: Invoice[]\\` @L27`\n\nThe code:\n\n```ts\nexport const invoice: Invoice[] = [];\n```\n\n**The type carries the intended meaning here too, and more strongly.** `Invoice[]` is load-bearing in a way `User` isn't: the `[]` initializer *only typechecks as an array*. Option (b) — narrowing to `invoice: Invoice` — doesn't just change intent, it breaks the file (`Type 'never[]' is not assignable to type 'Invoice'`), and you'd have to invent an invoice value to make it compile. That's a strong signal the array is real and the singular name is the defect.\n\nSame collision problem as above: `src/api/state.ts:34` already has `export const invoices: Invoice[] = [];`. So rename to something that distinguishes the two collections:\n\n```ts\nexport const pendingInvoices: Invoice[] = [];\n```\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n| Line | Evidence (verbatim) | Which side is right | Proposal |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 24 | `plural name, singular type: \\`users: User\\` @L24` | Type — value is one record | (a) rename → `currentUser` (`user` is taken at L33) |\n| 27 | `singular name, array type: \\`invoice: Invoice[]\\` @L27` | Type — `[]` only typechecks as array | (a) rename → `pendingInvoices` (`invoices` is taken at L34) |\n\nBoth land on option (a). That isn't a coincidence: when a type annotation and an initializer agree with each other and only the identifier disagrees, the name is the odd one out.\n\nTwo adjacent notes from the same scan worth carrying into any real fix:\n\n- The finding's own caveat — *\"v1 detector — type aliases and generic types are silently skipped\"* — means a `type UserList = User[]` or `Array<User>` annotation in this file would have gone unreported. Two flagged declarations is a floor, not a ceiling.\n- The `User` and `Invoice` interfaces these names point at (L4, L9) are *themselves* flagged as high-severity `contract_drift` against `src/billing.ts:4` and `src/billing.ts:19` (`crime_00011`, `crime_00012`) — the local `Invoice` is missing 6 fields including `userId`, `currency`, and three money fields. Renaming the binding makes the declaration honest about its shape; it does nothing about the type being a thin shadow of the real one.",
